        "at": "2026-11-16T22:31:53.262350Z",
        "re": "c1",
        "body": "Yes. Here substantive means the content meaning of a word, rather than the grammatical relation it marks. I have made the existing footnote and terminology entry more concrete, using the example to use, to employ. The later loss claim remains general; it does not assert that this particular verb sense had already disappeared."
      }
    }
  ],
  "replacements": [
    {
      "kind": "note",
      "id": "substantive-content",
      "evidence_id": "EFootnotes.note:substantive-content",
      "decision_id": "D031.note:substantive-content",
      "text": "“Substantive” means the content meaning of a word, as distinct from the grammatical relation it marks. For example, “to use, to employ” is the content meaning of the Chinese word also used as a preposition. An “original substantive meaning” is an earlier content meaning that may later be lost; “substantive” does not mean “noun.”"
    },
    {
      "kind": "guide",
      "id": "guide-8",
      "evidence_id": "EFootnotes.guide:guide-8",
      "decision_id": "D031.guide:guide-8",
      "text": "The contrast in 147 is between what a word means in its own right and a grammatical relation it expresses. The meaning “to use, to employ” is an example of content meaning; in a later stage of a word’s history, such an earlier meaning may be lost while a relational use remains. “Substantive” does not mean “noun,” and “relational word” is not imposed as a universal modern word-class label."
    }
  ],
